  "reference": "C_m = pitching moment / (q A d), A the maximum body cross-section and d the body diameter, 2.250 in; C_N on A (Symbols, printed p. 2). The moment center is at 63.37 and 66.36 percent of body length for configurations 1 and 2 (printed p. 2), 25.950 in and 35.500 in from the nose tip on Fig. 1(a) (printed p. 8). Positive C_m is nose-up: the report doesn't say so in words, but the fins-on curves of Fig. 5(b) fall to about 8 deg and then rise, which the text calls 'a severe pitch-up tendency' (printed p. 6), and with that sign the fins-on C_m over C_N reproduces Fig. 7's CP within 1.2% of body length (arcas-robin-wind-tunnel.json).",
  "reading": "Read from the report's plots, which print no tables, on 600-dpi renders (pdftoppm) of PDF pages 26 and 35 of the file pinned as nasa-tn-d-4014 in validation/refs.lock.toml (2026-09-19, M1.8e6), by the method of arcas-robin-high-alpha.json: each plot's grid fitted, each symbol located by fitting its outline and checked by eye; each Mach number's curve has its own zero, two C_m units apart. Seven symbols the automatic search misplaced (on a neighbouring curve, a grid line or where two curves cross) were placed by eye. Each symbol's angle agrees with the same run's on the C_N page within 0.04 deg rms (0.11 deg at worst). C_m +-0.02 (short) and +-0.025 (long); the report states +-0.05 for C_m itself (printed p. 5).",
